Yes, when assessing a victim for breathing, you should 'look, listen, and feel'. Visual inspection allows you to see chest movements, auscultation involves listening for breath sounds with a stethoscope, and palpation enables you to feel for air movement in the nostrils or mouth. These methods together can help you determine if the victim is breathing.
